@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Theming}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Accessibility}")]
@implements IAsyncDisposable

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Theming_Name @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Theming_Name @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</h1>

<p>
    The border and focus indicator colours in the library (css variable values/tokens) were calculated to meet <b>WCAG 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast (AA)</b>, 
    which requires a 3:1 contrast ratio between a component's boundary and adjacent colours, and <b>WCAG 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum) (AA)</b> for text, 
    which requires 4.5:1 (3:1 for large text). Because the palette is generated from HSL lightness offsets relative to 
    <span class="code-span">--br-unit-colour-primary</span> (css colour functions), rather than fixed hex values, these ratios have some built-in latitude.
    When you change the primary colour, the derived shades scale proportionally so in a lot of cases the WCAG requirements may still be met. 
    This isn't guaranteed but it means a lot of colours will "just work" without needing to change the underlying css variable values.
</p>

<p>
    This pages lists the darkest and lightest backgrounds used in the component library so if the primary colour is changed you can see which
    values may need adjusting for the components.
</p>
<p>
    <b>WCAG 2.4.13 Focus Appearance (AAA)</b>, which asks clear indication of an item that has focus. This library uses a focus ring of 2px and in most cases, 
    with a slight offset. 
</p>

<p>
    Below are the items/CSS variables that sit closest to the boundaries of the requirements given their calculated shades, and are the ones I'd recommend 
    to check the contrast for after changing the primary colour.
</p>

<p>
    On page initialisation (and after colour change), the current computed CSS custom property values are read directly from the page and checked for contrast. 
    If any of the shown variables fail, you don't need to touch the library itself — just override that variable in your own <span class="code-span">:root</span> block, declared 
    after the Blazor Ramp stylesheet so it takes precedence in the cascade, and point it to a different step on the existing scale rather than a new hard-coded colour. 
    Stepping to the next darker/lighter value on the scale is usually enough to clear the threshold.
</p>
<p>
    The browsers default colour picker below is bound to the primary colour, so just change it to change the entire site so you can go look at any component and use the
    dark theme switch at the top of the screen to see what everything looks like. Please note, there is no function that automatically picks the dark theme values, I just selected
    them but I think these would be satisfy a lot of applications. You can see what I used on the Theming overview page and I will be adding an export/copy to clipboard function on
    the sandbox page (once completed) for you to get all the variables for your sites style sheet.
</p>
<p>
    The neutral tinge slider below only goes from 0 to 15 (the library default is 10), but as the underlying CSS colour function uses a percentage you could go higher but you really then would not have neutrals.
</p>
<p>
    <b>Note: </b> I often change the primary colour of this site and the test site from the default. Also note given that I have to get the computed
    css values (given the css colour functions), apply them to a canvas, convert them to hex/RGB for my colour contrast utility
    there may be slight rounding differences to other checkers by about 0.01 on some colours.
</p>

<ThemeColourTool OnColourChanged="HandleColourChanged" />
<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> Contrast ratios unlike the colours will only update on final selection.
</p>
